WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.042
<v Examiner>Four marks.

2
00:00:02.042 --> 00:00:05.200
<v Examiner>Describe the difference between acute and chronic pain,

3
00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:08.301
<v Examiner>and explain how a nurse would assess a patient's pain.

4
00:00:08.751 --> 00:00:10.172
The definition first.

5
00:00:10.172 --> 00:00:15.585
Pain is an unpleasant sensory and emotional experience associated with actual or

6
00:00:15.585 --> 00:00:17.209
potential tissue damage.

7
00:00:17.209 --> 00:00:21.337
And the line to remember: pain is what the person says it is.

8
00:00:21.787 --> 00:00:27.523
Acute pain has a rapid onset, lasts less than six months, and resolves as the area heals,

9
00:00:27.523 --> 00:00:29.069
like pain after surgery.

10
00:00:29.069 --> 00:00:31.647
Chronic pain lasts more than six months,

11
00:00:31.647 --> 00:00:35.836
has a cause that isn't life-threatening and is sometimes unknown,

12
00:00:35.836 --> 00:00:40.411
like arthritis or back pain, and it can lead to depression and anxiety.

13
00:00:40.861 --> 00:00:42.974
The types, for the multi-choice.

14
00:00:42.974 --> 00:00:45.747
Nociceptive pain comes from tissue damage.

15
00:00:45.747 --> 00:00:49.180
Somatic pain is localised: sharp, aching, throbbing.

16
00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:53.471
Visceral pain is diffuse or referred: crampy, squeezing, colicky.

17
00:00:53.471 --> 00:00:59.347
Neuropathic pain comes from a lesion or disease of the nervous system: shooting, burning,

18
00:00:59.347 --> 00:01:01.789
numbness, or pain from a light touch.

19
00:01:02.239 --> 00:01:03.410
Now how to assess.

20
00:01:03.410 --> 00:01:04.321
Use OLD CARTS.

21
00:01:04.321 --> 00:01:05.947
Onset: when did it start?

22
00:01:05.947 --> 00:01:07.378
Location: where is it?

23
00:01:07.378 --> 00:01:09.459
Duration: how long does it last?

24
00:01:09.459 --> 00:01:12.061
Characteristics: what does it feel like?

25
00:01:12.061 --> 00:01:14.727
Aggravating factors: what makes it worse?

26
00:01:14.727 --> 00:01:17.329
Relieving factors: what makes it better?

27
00:01:17.329 --> 00:01:19.345
Treatment: what have you tried?

28
00:01:19.345 --> 00:01:20.906
Severity: how bad is it?

29
00:01:20.906 --> 00:01:25.589
Use a scale: zero to ten for most adults, asked at rest and on movement.

30
00:01:25.589 --> 00:01:29.491
For children, people who can't speak, or a language barrier,

31
00:01:29.491 --> 00:01:31.508
use the Wong-Baker faces scale.

32
00:01:31.508 --> 00:01:35.215
Ask the patient what level of pain is acceptable to them,

33
00:01:35.215 --> 00:01:37.947
meaning able to do their daily activities.

34
00:01:37.947 --> 00:01:39.443
And when do you assess?

35
00:01:39.443 --> 00:01:41.849
Every time you check on your patient.

36
00:01:41.849 --> 00:01:47.313
The first thing to do when someone complains of pain is assess it with a pain scale.

37
00:01:47.763 --> 00:01:50.250
Pain is what the person says it is.

38
00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:54.513
Acute: rapid onset, under six months, resolves with healing.

39
00:01:54.513 --> 00:01:58.705
Chronic: over six months, can bring depression and anxiety.

40
00:01:58.705 --> 00:02:05.100
Assess with OLD CARTS: onset, location, duration, characteristics, aggravating, relieving,

41
00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:06.521
treatment, severity.

42
00:02:06.521 --> 00:02:09.506
Zero-to-ten scale at rest and on movement;

43
00:02:09.506 --> 00:02:13.201
Wong-Baker faces for children or a language barrier.
